<h2>The applications</h2>
<p>The prosecution under Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 concerned two cheques presented in March 2019 and returned for insufficiency of funds. Five years into the proceedings, the accused moved the trial court to summon the bank manager, contending that the manager\'s evidence would show that cheques of the State Bank of Bikaner and Jaipur ceased to be valid on the bank\'s merger with the State Bank of India, and that no amount had in fact been debited from the account. The trial court declined; the accused invoked the High Court\'s supervisory jurisdiction.</p>

<h2>The High Court\'s reasoning</h2>
<p>The court dismissed both petitions on a clean necessity analysis. The legal effect of the merger is a question of law — it can be urged at final arguments, supported if needed by judicial notice under Section 57 of the Evidence Act, without any witness deposing to it. The state of the account is a matter of record — account statements and bank documents already available, or obtainable through the documentary route, establish whether funds moved. Nothing the bank manager could say would add an essential fact that the record could not. The trial court, the High Court added, remains free to address the validity questions at the stage of final arguments.</p>
<div class="note"><p>The subtext is the special character of NI Act trials. Chapter XVII prosecutions are meant to be summary and swift; Section 143 of the NI Act itself directs day-to-day trial where practicable. Courts are correspondingly alert to applications that would convert a documents-driven case into a parade of formal witnesses.</p></div>

<h2>The necessity test in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h4>Essential, not useful</h4><p>The touchstone is whether the evidence is essential to a just decision — not whether it might be marginally useful or would make the record tidier.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Documents before witnesses</h4><p>Where certified records under the Bankers\' Books Evidence Act or exhibited statements prove the point, oral testimony from the banker adds nothing but time.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Law is argued</h4><p>Propositions of law — the effect of a merger, the validity of an instrument — are for submissions, and where appropriate judicial notice, not for a witness box.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Stage and diligence</h4><p>A recall or summoning plea made years into the trial, on material available all along, invites the inference of delay — and courts act on that inference.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Takeaways for cheque litigation</h2>
<p>For the defence, the decision is a caution against holding back structural arguments — if the instrument\'s validity or the account\'s state is the defence, build it through documents early, and keep the legal argument for final hearing rather than manufacturing a witness requirement late in the day. For complainants, it is a tool: oppose belated Section 311 (now Section 348 BNSS) applications by showing that every fact sought is already provable on the record, and remind the court of the NI Act\'s summary design. Delhi\'s Magistrates\' courts, which carry one of the country\'s heaviest cheque-case dockets, apply precisely this discipline — and this order supplies a current, citable illustration of it.</p>
<p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice or a solicitation.</p>
